    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11625-017-0525-2",
    "abstract": "We investigate how uncertainties in key parameters in the carbon cycle and climate system propagate to the costs of climate change mitigation and adaptation needed to achieve the 2 and 1.5 \u00b0C targets by 2100 using a stochastic version of the simple climate model for optimization (SCM4OPT), an integrated assessment model. For the 2 \u00b0C target, we find a difference in 2100 CO<sub>2</sub> emission levels of 20.5 GtCO<sub>2</sub> (- 1.2 GtCO<sub>2</sub> to 19.4 GtCO<sub>2</sub>), whereas this difference is 12.0 GtCO<sub>2</sub> (- 6.9 GtCO<sub>2</sub> to 5.1 GtCO<sub>2</sub>) for the 1.5 \u00b0C target (17-83% range). Total radiative forcing in 2100 is estimated to be 3.3 (2.7-3.9) Wm<sup>-2</sup> for the 2 \u00b0C case and 2.5 (2.0-3.0) Wm<sup>-2</sup> for the 1.5 \u00b0C case. Carbon prices in 2100 are 482 (181-732) USD(2005)/tCO<sub>2</sub> and 713 (498-1014) USD(2005)/tCO<sub>2</sub> for the 2 and 1.5 \u00b0C targets, respectively. We estimate GDP losses in 2100 that correspond to 1.9 (1.2-2.5)% of total gross output for the 2 \u00b0C target and 2.0 (1.5-2.7)% for the 1.5 \u00b0C target.",
    "field": "Integrated assessment of climate change",
    "problem": "Existing Integrated Assessment Models often neglect key uncertainties from the carbon cycle and aerosols, focusing primarily on climate sensitivity, which hinders a complete evaluation of the emission pathways and socioeconomic costs required to meet 1.5 \u00b0C and 2 \u00b0C climate targets.",
    "method": "The authors employ a stochastic integrated assessment model (SCM4OPT) to investigate how uncertainties in carbon cycle and climate system parameters propagate to the emission pathways and socioeconomic costs required to achieve the 2 and 1.5 \u00b0C climate targets.",
    "task": "The task is to investigate how uncertainties in the carbon cycle and climate system propagate to the socioeconomic costs and emission pathways required to achieve the 2 and 1.5 \u00b0C climate targets, using an integrated assessment model.",
